// A self-contained Gmail tool set — pure Katari, no sidecar: every Gmail API call is `http.fetch` with the
// request built and the reply parsed as `json` in Katari, and authentication is the runtime's credentials
// core, reached through `oauth.token`. The tools an app hands to the model (`list_recent` / `read_body` /
// `send` / `modify_labels`), a notification watcher (`watch`) that composes under `parallel [ … ]`, and the
// provider that makes wiring it a one-liner: `use gmail.provider(source = credentials.oauth(name = "google"))`.
//
// Authentication is identical to the `google_calendar` package (a shared Google OAuth credential): the
// provider holds NO secret and keeps NO cache. It takes a credential NAME and serves `credential` by
// resolving that name through the runtime (`oauth.token`) — the operator registers the Google OAuth client
// once (with a Gmail scope) and logs in through the runtime-hosted browser flow, after which the runtime
// owns the token material end to end. The resolved access token is a `string of private` — it flows only
// toward the `Authorization: Bearer` header and is redacted at every user-facing boundary.
//
// Failure has THREE meanings, each decided at its own boundary. (1) A credential that needs a HUMAN —
// never authorized, or refresh-dead — is never an error: `oauth.token` PARKS the run on a
// `prelude.oauth.authorize` escalation and resumes once the browser flow completes; this package neither
// sees nor classifies that state. (2) A transient token-resolution failure (a network error, a
// token-endpoint 5xx) is `oauth.server_error`, thrown at the provider. (3) The Gmail API's own failures are
// this package's two-variant sum (`gmail_error = auth_error | api_error`), classified once in
// `classify_api_error`: a 401/403 is `auth_error` (Google rejected the resolved token — a replay
// re-resolves it, and a credential the runtime cannot refresh parks as a re-authorization prompt), every
// other non-2xx is `api_error` (a bad request, a quota or server error — a plain backoff's case).
//
// The `watch` daemon is built on `time.watch` with an interval schedule, NOT a `time.sleep` recursion: a
// per-poll recursion accumulates a durable delegation frame per tick, growing without bound over a daemon's
// lifetime, whereas `time.watch`'s reactor owns the loop and ticks add no depth. The dedup memory — the set
// of already-delivered message ids — rides a handler `var`, committed PER DELIVERED MESSAGE
// (`deliver_if_new`) and pruned each tick to the ids the query still returns (`retain_window`), so the
// memory stays bounded to one poll's result and a mid-tick failure never re-delivers a message already
// delivered in that tick. Delivery is AT-LEAST-ONCE — exactly-once per message id only WITHIN one watch
// activation.
//
// Both directions use the stdlib string codecs added for exactly this (`string.to_base64url` /
// `string.from_base64url` / `string.url_encode`): `send` base64url-encodes the RFC822 message into the
// ordinary `messages.send` `raw` field, `read_body` base64url-decodes the `text/plain` part's `body.data`
// to real text (falling back to the snippet only when a message has no plain-text part), and the `q`
// query is percent-encoded in full.

@"An authentication failure: a Gmail API call came back 401/403 — Google rejected the access token the
runtime resolved (revoked before its stored expiry, or scoped too narrowly for the call). Retrying the SAME
token does not help, but replaying the call recovers without any app-owned escalation: catch it in a
converter that signals `replay.interrupted` — the re-run asks `credential` afresh, the runtime refreshes the
credential once its stored lifetime passes, and a credential the runtime cannot refresh parks the run on a
`prelude.oauth.authorize` escalation (the admin console's re-authorization prompt) instead of failing.
`message` carries the status and the server's body."
data auth_error(message: string)

@"A Gmail API call failed for a non-authentication reason: the endpoint returned a non-2xx status that is
not 401/403 (a bad request, a not-found message, a quota or server error). `message` carries the status and
the server's body so the model can read what went wrong and adjust. Distinct from `auth_error` so a program
can tell a request the service faulted on (back off and replay as-is) from a token Google rejected (the
replay's re-resolution is what fixes it)."
data api_error(message: string)

// A Gmail failure: an authentication failure or a plain API failure. (Type synonyms take no docs; classified
// once in `classify_api_error` — 401/403 is `auth_error`, everything else `api_error`.)
type gmail_error = auth_error | api_error

@"A read message trimmed to what an assistant needs: the @id@ (the Gmail API message id — addresses
`read_body` / `modify_labels`), the @thread_id@ (the conversation it belongs to — pass it to `send` to
reply inside the same thread), the @message_id@ (the RFC822 `Message-ID` header, e.g.
\"<abc@mail.gmail.com>\" — DISTINCT from @id@; pass it to `send`'s @in_reply_to@ so a reply threads in
every mail client), the @sender@ (the raw `From` header), the @subject@ line, the @snippet@ (Gmail's short
plain-text preview of the message) and the @date@ (the raw `Date` header, e.g.
\"Mon, 21 Jul 2026 09:15:00 +0900\"). (The sender field is `sender`, not `from`, because `from` is a
reserved word.)"
data message(id: string, thread_id: string, message_id: string, sender: string, subject: string, snippet: string, date: string)

// ── the provider (the credential-name capability) ────────────────────────────

@"Get a usable Google access token, served by `provider` for the extent of a continuation — the capability
the tools require (`with … credential`). Each ask resolves the provider's named credential through the
runtime (`oauth.token`): the runtime returns the stored token while its recorded lifetime holds and refreshes
it against the stored token endpoint once due, so no token bookkeeping lives in the program. The token is a
`string of private` — it flows only toward a submission sink (the `Bearer` header). A credential that needs a
human (never authorized, or refresh-dead) never throws: the ask PARKS the run on a `prelude.oauth.authorize`
escalation and resumes once the browser flow completes. A transient resolution failure throws
`oauth.server_error` at the provider."
request credential() -> string of private

// On a downstream 401/403 (an access token revoked BEFORE its stored expiry): there is no invalidation
// channel here, and none is needed. The runtime keeps serving the stored token while its clock says valid, so
// a mid-lifetime revocation surfaces as `auth_error` on every call for up to one access-token lifetime (≈ an
// hour for Google) — the bounded worst case. A converter that turns `auth_error` into `replay.interrupted`
// makes the recovery automatic: each re-run asks `credential` afresh, the resolution refreshes once the stored
// lifetime passes, and a refresh the token endpoint refuses parks the run as a re-authorization prompt.
@"Provide the Google credential for the extent of the continuation, from @source@ — the
credentials-unification shape: `use gmail.provider(source = credentials.oauth(name = \"google\"))`
(the same stored Google credential the `google_calendar` package uses, given a Gmail scope). Resolution
happens on EVERY `credential` ask (the runtime owns the token material, its cache and its refresh, so this
provider holds no secret and keeps no state); an unauthorized oauth credential parks the run on a
`prelude.oauth.authorize` escalation rather than failing, and a transient resolution failure throws at the
asking call."
agent provider[R, effect E](
  source: credentials.source,
  continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, credential},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[env.missing_secret | oauth.server_error] {
  use handler {
    request credential() -> string of private {
      next credentials.resolve(source = source)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"The `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header every Gmail API call carries. The token is a
`string of private` (the runtime-resolved credential); concatenation propagates the taint, and the header
record is a private-capable submission sink, so the secret leaves only toward Google's server."
agent bearer(token: string of private) -> record[string of private] {
  { "Authorization" = "Bearer " ++ token }
}

@"Classify a failed Gmail API response into the error sum, at the ONE boundary where the split is decided.
A 401 or 403 is an authentication failure — Google rejected the resolved access token (revoked, or scoped
too narrowly) — surfaced as `auth_error`, which a replay converter turns into a fresh token resolution (and,
when the credential is refresh-dead, the runtime's re-authorization park). Every other non-2xx is an
`api_error`. (403 is read as auth even though Google also returns it for quota exhaustion; the
access-rejection reading is the one worth composing the re-resolution around, and a quota 403 that is
replayed simply fails again.) @context@ names the call for the message."
agent classify_api_error(context: string, status: integer, body: string) -> gmail_error {
  let status_text = string.to_string(value = status)
  let message = f"${context} failed (status ${status_text}): ${body}"
  match (status) {
    case 401 -> auth_error(message = message)
    case 403 -> auth_error(message = message)
    case _ -> api_error(message = message)
  }
}

// ── shared Gmail GET ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Issue an authenticated GET to a Gmail API @url@ and return its parsed JSON body — the one place the
`Bearer` header, the 2xx check and the error classification live, shared by every read (`list_recent`,
`read_body`, `get_message`, and `watch`'s poll). A non-2xx becomes `classify_api_error(@context@, ...)`
(401/403 → `auth_error`, else `api_error`); @context@ names the caller for the message."
agent gmail_get(url: string, context: string) -> unknown {
  let response = http.fetch(
    url = url,
    method = "GET",
    headers = bearer(token = credential()),
    body = http.text(content = ""),
  )
  if (response.status >= 200 && response.status < 300) {
    json.parse(text = response.body)
  } else {
    prelude.throw(error = classify_api_error(context = context, status = response.status, body = response.body))
  }
}

// ── response parsing (read out of json) ──────────────────────────────────────

@"The value of the first message header named @name@ (matched case-insensitively) in the @headers@ array of
`{name, value}` objects, or the empty string when absent. Gmail returns each requested metadata header as
such an object; this is the total lookup `render_message` reads From / Subject / Date through."
agent header_value(headers: array[unknown], name: string) -> string {
  let lowered = string.to_lower(value = name)
  let matches = array.flatten(target = for (let entry in headers) {
    next if (string.to_lower(value = json.text(target = json.field(target = entry, key = "name"))) == lowered) {
      [json.text(target = json.field(target = entry, key = "value"))]
    } else {
      []
    }
  })
  match (array.get(target = matches, index = 0)) {
    case null -> ""
    case value -> value
  }
}

@"One Gmail message resource as a `message`. The From / Subject / Date / Message-ID come from the
`payload.headers` array (looked up by name via `header_value`); the id, thread id and snippet come off the
resource directly. Every read is totalized — a missing header or field degrades to the empty string, so a
partial resource never throws into `watch`'s loop."
agent render_message(resource: unknown) -> message {
  let headers = json.items(target = json.field(target = json.field(target = resource, key = "payload"), key = "headers"))
  message(
    id = json.text(target = json.field(target = resource, key = "id")),
    thread_id = json.text(target = json.field(target = resource, key = "threadId")),
    message_id = header_value(headers = headers, name = "Message-ID"),
    sender = header_value(headers = headers, name = "From"),
    subject = header_value(headers = headers, name = "Subject"),
    snippet = json.text(target = json.field(target = resource, key = "snippet")),
    date = header_value(headers = headers, name = "Date"),
  )
}

@"Fetch one message's salient fields by @id@ — a `messages.get` with `format=metadata` asking only for the
From / Subject / Date / Message-ID headers, plus the snippet the resource always carries. The shared read
behind `list_recent` and `watch`'s delivery; totalized through `render_message`."
agent get_message(id: string) -> message {
  render_message(resource = gmail_get(
    url = f"https://gmail.googleapis.com/gmail/v1/users/me/messages/${id}?format=metadata&metadataHeaders=From&metadataHeaders=Subject&metadataHeaders=Date&metadataHeaders=Message-ID",
    context = "get_message",
  ))
}

@"Percent-encode a Gmail search @value@ for the `q` URL parameter — a full component encoder
(`string.url_encode`), so a quoted phrase, an `&`, or any reserved character in the query rides
intact, not just spaces."
agent encode_query(value: string) -> string {
  string.url_encode(value = value)
}

@"List the ids of the messages matching a Gmail search @query@, newest first, at most @max_results@ — a
`messages.list`, which returns only `{id, threadId}` per hit (the salient fields need a `get_message` each).
A reply without the `messages` array (no match) degrades to the empty list. Shared by `list_recent` and
`watch`'s poll."
agent list_message_ids(query: string, max_results: integer) -> array[string] {
  let parsed = gmail_get(
    url = f"https://gmail.googleapis.com/gmail/v1/users/me/messages?q=${encode_query(value = query)}&maxResults=${string.to_string(value = max_results)}",
    context = "list_message_ids",
  )
  for (let item in json.items(target = json.field(target = parsed, key = "messages"))) {
    next json.text(target = json.field(target = item, key = "id"))
  }
}

// ── the tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Tool: list the most recent messages matching a Gmail search @query@ — the same query language as the
Gmail search box (\"is:unread\", \"from:alice@example.com newer_than:2d\", \"subject:invoice\") — newest
first, at most @max_results@. Each result is trimmed to id / thread / sender / subject / snippet / date, small
enough to feed straight back to the model, and its @id@ addresses `read_body` and `modify_labels`. A message
resource is fetched per id for its headers, so a large @max_results@ is a proportional number of calls."
agent list_recent(query: string, max_results: integer ?= 10) -> array[message] {
  for (let id in list_message_ids(query = query, max_results = max_results)) {
    next get_message(id = id)
  }
}

@"Tool: read a message's plain-text body by @id@. Fetches the full message and returns its decoded
`text/plain` body — the `payload.body.data` (or, for a `multipart/*` message, the first `text/plain`
descendant's `body.data`) base64url-decoded to real text. A message with no plain-text part anywhere (an
HTML-only mail) has nothing to decode, so the tool falls back to Gmail's SNIPPET — the short plain-text
preview the API carries on every resource — which is total and always readable."
agent read_body(id: string) -> string {
  let resource = gmail_get(
    url = f"https://gmail.googleapis.com/gmail/v1/users/me/messages/${id}?format=full",
    context = "read_body",
  )
  let decoded = decode_plain_text(payload = json.field(target = resource, key = "payload"))
  // A message with no text/plain part (an HTML-only mail, an empty walk) falls back to the snippet
  // Gmail always carries, so the tool is total and always returns something readable.
  if (string.is_blank(value = decoded)) {
    json.text(target = json.field(target = resource, key = "snippet"))
  } else {
    decoded
  }
}

@"The decoded text/plain body of a Gmail @payload@ — its `body.data` (base64url) decoded when the
part is `text/plain`, otherwise the first `text/plain` descendant found by walking `parts` (a
`multipart/*` message). Total: no text/plain part anywhere returns the empty string. Internal to
`read_body`."
agent decode_plain_text(payload: unknown) -> string with pure {
  match (json.field(target = payload, key = "mimeType")) {
    case string(mime_type) -> {
      if (mime_type == "text/plain") {
        string.from_base64url(value = json.text(target = json.field(target = json.field(target = payload, key = "body"), key = "data")))
      } else {
        // A multipart (or any container) message: walk the parts and take the first plain-text body.
        let found = for (let part in json.items(target = json.field(target = payload, key = "parts"))) {
          next decode_plain_text(payload = part)
        }
        let non_empty = array.flatten(target = for (let text in found) {
          next if (string.is_blank(value = text)) { [] } else { [text] }
        })
        match (array.get(target = non_empty, index = 0)) {
          case null -> ""
          case first -> first
        }
      }
    }
    case _ -> ""
  }
}

@"Tool: send a plain-text email from the authenticated user. @to@ is the recipient address, @subject@ the
subject line, @body@ the message body. Returns the sent message's id. To REPLY inside a conversation, pass
@thread_id@ (a message's `thread_id`, so Gmail files the reply in that thread) and @in_reply_to@ (the
message's `message_id` — its RFC822 `Message-ID` header, read from `list_recent`); both default empty for a
fresh send. When @in_reply_to@ is given it becomes the reply's `In-Reply-To` and `References` headers, so a
mail client threads it too. (Compose the @subject@ yourself — prefix \"Re: \" for a reply.) The RFC822
message is base64url-encoded into the ordinary `messages.send` `raw` field. Raises `gmail_error`
(`auth_error` | `api_error`) on a non-2xx reply."
agent send(to: string, subject: string, body: string, thread_id: string ?= "", in_reply_to: string ?= "") -> string {
  // A reply carries `In-Reply-To` / `References` naming the parent's RFC822 Message-ID, so a mail client
  // threads it (Gmail also threads server-side via `threadId` below). Empty `in_reply_to` (a fresh send)
  // adds no such headers. Since the parent's own References chain is not exposed, `References` is just the
  // parent — RFC 5322's accepted fallback when the ancestor chain is unknown.
  let reply_headers = match (in_reply_to) {
    case "" -> ""
    case parent -> f"In-Reply-To: ${parent}\r\nReferences: ${parent}\r\n"
  }
  // An RFC 5322 message: the To / Subject (and any reply) headers, a blank line, then the body. CRLF
  // separates header lines and the header/body boundary, as the mail format requires.
  let raw = f"To: ${to}\r\nSubject: ${subject}\r\n${reply_headers}\r\n${body}"
  // The ordinary `messages.send` with the message base64url-encoded in the `raw` field — the standard
  // path, now that `string.to_base64url` exists. `http.fetch` (not `post_json`) keeps the custom
  // 401/403 → `auth_error` classification, like every other write here.
  let base: record[unknown] = { raw = string.to_base64url(value = raw) }
  // `threadId` files the message in an existing conversation; omitted (empty `thread_id`) it starts a new
  // one, so an omitted arg is a smaller request rather than a blank field.
  let payload = match (thread_id) {
    case "" -> base
    case conversation -> record.set(target = base, key = "threadId", value = conversation)
  }
  let response = http.fetch(
    url = "https://gmail.googleapis.com/gmail/v1/users/me/messages/send",
    method = "POST",
    headers = bearer(token = credential()),
    body = http.json(value = payload),
  )
  if (response.status >= 200 && response.status < 300) {
    json.text(target = json.field(target = json.parse(text = response.body), key = "id"))
  } else {
    prelude.throw(error = classify_api_error(context = "send", status = response.status, body = response.body))
  }
}

@"Tool: change a message's labels — add the label ids in @add@, remove those in @remove@. The common use is
marking a message read by removing \"UNREAD\", or filing it by adding a label id. Both lists default to empty
(an empty call is a no-op at the API). @id@ is the message id. Returns null; raises `gmail_error`
(`auth_error` | `api_error`) on a non-2xx reply."
agent modify_labels(id: string, add: array[string] ?= [], remove: array[string] ?= []) -> null {
  let payload: record[unknown] = { addLabelIds = add, removeLabelIds = remove }
  // `http.json` serialises the value tree and defaults the application/json Content-Type; the custom
  // 401/403 → `auth_error` classification is why this keeps `http.fetch` rather than `post_json`.
  let response = http.fetch(
    url = f"https://gmail.googleapis.com/gmail/v1/users/me/messages/${id}/modify",
    method = "POST",
    headers = bearer(token = credential()),
    body = http.json(value = payload),
  )
  if (response.status >= 200 && response.status < 300) {
    null
  } else {
    prelude.throw(error = classify_api_error(context = "modify_labels", status = response.status, body = response.body))
  }
}

// ── the watch daemon ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Deliver one message and record its id the INSTANT its `deliver_to` returns — the per-message dedup
commit. Handled inside `watch`; the id is looked up in the durable `already_notified` var (absent → fetch
the message, deliver, and append; present → skip), so a mid-tick failure never re-delivers a message already
delivered in the same tick. The message resource is fetched lazily HERE (only for a new id), so a poll that
finds nothing new issues no per-message reads. Internal to `watch`; an app does not handle it."
request deliver_if_new(id: string) -> null

@"Prune the dedup memory to @keys@ — the ids the query returned this poll — dropping messages that have
left the result set (read, aged out of a `newer_than:` window, or fallen past the poll cap), so the memory
stays bounded to one poll. Performed once per tick before delivery. Internal to `watch`; an app does not
handle it."
request retain_window(keys: array[string]) -> null

@"Watch the mailbox for messages matching a Gmail search @query@ and deliver each NEW one, once per
activation, to @deliver_to@ — shaped like `time.watch` / `discord.watch_messages` so it composes under
`parallel [ … ]`. Every @poll_interval_milliseconds@ it lists the most recent messages matching @query@
(e.g. \"is:unread\" or \"newer_than:1d\") and calls @deliver_to@ for each id not yet notified. @deliver_to@'s
effects @E@ flow to the caller's handlers unchanged, so a delivery may itself call other tools (reply,
label). `watch` never resolves (`-> never`); it runs until the run is cancelled.

Delivery is AT-LEAST-ONCE, exactly-once per message id only WITHIN a single activation: a message that keeps
matching @query@ is delivered a single time while the watch keeps running. A crash-recovery restart resumes
the durable dedup memory and does not re-notify; but a replay re-run (a `replay.forever` / `replay.exponential`
around the watch re-invokes its continuation, giving a fresh activation whose memory starts empty)
re-delivers every message still matching, and a `deliver_to` failure re-delivers the messages after it in
that tick on the next run. The dedup memory is pruned each tick to the ids the query still returns, so a
message that leaves the result set (it was read, or aged out of a `newer_than:` window) drops from memory —
and if it re-enters the result later it re-notifies. The poll returns the most recent matches up to an
internal cap, so a burst of more new matches than the cap between two polls delays the oldest until they
re-surface within the cap.

No retry is built in — a poll failure (an http error, a rejected token, a `deliver_to` that throws)
propagates and kills the watch, exactly as an uncaught failure in any callee does. Compose resilience by
wrapping the watch in a replay provider (`replay.forever` with a converter that replays the transient blips
AND `auth_error` — the re-run re-resolves the token, and a credential needing a human parks on the runtime's
authorize escalation rather than failing). The first poll is one @poll_interval@ in (a `time.watch`
occurrence fires one interval after the start)."
agent watch[effect E](
  query: string,
  poll_interval_milliseconds: number,
  deliver_to: agent (message: message) -> null with E,
) -> never with E | io | credential | prelude.throw[gmail_error | http.fetch_error | json.parse_error] {
  // The dedup memory is a handler `var` (durable across a restart). `retain_window` prunes it to the ids the
  // query still returns each tick and `deliver_if_new` advances it once per delivered message, so
  // `time.watch` owns the loop and the memory — not a growing recursion — is the only state carried between
  // polls.
  use handler (var already_notified: array[string] = []) {
    request retain_window(keys: array[string]) {
      // Keep only the ids still in this poll's result; a message that has left it drops out, bounding the
      // memory to one poll's size.
      let retained = array.flatten(target = for (let key in already_notified) {
        next if (array.contains(target = keys, value = key)) { [key] } else { [] }
      })
      next null with { already_notified = retained }
    }
    request deliver_if_new(id: string) {
      // The dedup key is the message id (stable and globally unique in the mailbox). The message is fetched
      // and delivered only for an unseen id, and the commit happens AFTER `deliver_to` returns, so a throw
      // leaves the id unmarked and the message is retried on a later run.
      match (array.index_of(target = already_notified, value = id)) {
        case null -> {
          deliver_to(message = get_message(id = id))
          next null with { already_notified = array.append(target = already_notified, value = id) }
        }
        case _ -> { next null }
      }
    }
  }
  // The `time.watch` deliver_to: list this tick's matches, reconcile the dedup memory to them, then deliver
  // the unseen messages one at a time. A local agent because it captures `query`; its `retain_window` /
  // `deliver_if_new` are handled by the var above, its `credential` / throws flow out to the caller. The
  // occurrence's scheduled instant (`time`) is unused — this watch selects by query, not by a time window.
  agent poll_on_tick(time: number) -> null with credential | io | retain_window | deliver_if_new | prelude.throw[gmail_error | http.fetch_error | json.parse_error] {
    // Cap the poll at a bounded window of recent matches; the dedup memory is pruned to exactly this set, so
    // the cap is also the memory bound.
    let ids = list_message_ids(query = query, max_results = 50)
    retain_window(keys = ids)
    for (let id in ids) { deliver_if_new(id = id) }
    null
  }
  time.watch(schedule = time.interval(milliseconds = poll_interval_milliseconds), deliver_to = poll_on_tick)
}
